answer:
The structure:

struct LibraryBook {
    char title[40];
    char author[40];
    int copies;
};

Note the semicolon after the closing brace, which the declaration requires.

The statements:

struct LibraryBook b;
strcpy(b.title, "Things Fall Apart");
strcpy(b.author, "Chinua Achebe");
b.copies = 3;
printf("%s: %d copies\n", b.title, b.copies);

The first line defines the variable and reserves its storage. Members are reached with the dot, both for writing and for reading. The two character arrays cannot be filled with =, because an array name is not something you can assign into, so their contents are copied in with strcpy; copies is a plain int and takes an ordinary assignment. The conversion specifiers follow the member types, %s for the arrays and %d for the int.

Size: 40 + 40 + 4 = 84 bytes. Each member of a structure gets its own storage, laid out one after another, so the size of the whole is the sum of the sizes of its members: two forty-byte character arrays and a four-byte int.

If struct were replaced by union, the layout collapses onto itself. All three members would begin at the same address, so title, author and copies would be three names for one block of memory rather than three separate blocks, and the size would become 40 bytes, the size of the largest member, because a union is only ever as big as its biggest member. The statements would then destroy one another in sequence: strcpy(b.title, ...) writes the title into the block, strcpy(b.author, ...) writes the author over the top of it and the title is gone, and b.copies = 3 then writes a four-byte integer over the first bytes of the author text. The printf would print rubbish for the title, since it prints whatever bytes are sitting at the start of the shared block, and 3 for the copies, because that was written last. A union variable can hold a valid value for only one member at a time, so a LibraryBook, which has to have a title and an author and a count all at once, must be a structure; a union is only right where the members are alternatives that never need to coexist.

struct LibraryBook { char title[40]; char author[40]; int copies; };  <- semicolon at the end

struct LibraryBook b;
strcpy(b.title, "Things Fall Apart");
strcpy(b.author, "Chinua Achebe");
b.copies = 3;
printf("%s: %d copies\n", b.title, b.copies);

dot gets u the member both for setting and reading. cant do b.title = "..." coz its an array so u use strcpy, copies is just an int so plain = is fine. %s for the two arrays %d for the int.

size is 40+40+4 = 84 bytes, evry member gets its own space one after the other so u just add them up.

if its a union instead: all 3 start at the same adress so theyre 3 names for the same block not 3 blocks, and the size drops to 40 which is just the biggest member. the statements then wreck each other, title goes in, author lands on top of it and the title is gone, then copies=3 puts an int over the first bytes of the author. printf prints garbage for title and 3 for copies coz copies went in last. a book needs title AND author AND count at the same time so it has to be a struct, union is only for things that are alternatives.

struct LibraryBook { char title[40]; char author[40]; int copies; };

struct LibraryBook b;
strcpy(b.title, "Things Fall Apart");
strcpy(b.author, "Chinua Achebe");
b.copies = 3;
printf("%s: %d copies\n", b.title, b.copies);

The little full stop between b and the name of the thing you want is how you get at any one of the three, whether you are putting something in or taking something out. You cannot just put an equals sign after the title and hand it a string, because a run of letters like that is a fixed row of pigeonholes and you are not allowed to swap the whole row for a different row, you have to go and put the letters in one by one, which is the job strcpy does for you. The number of copies is a single value sitting on its own, so an equals sign is fine there. When printing, you have to tell it which kind of thing it is looking at, letters or a number, hence the two different markers in the printing line.

For the size: the row for the title is 40 pigeonholes, the row for the author is another 40, and the number takes 4, and since each of the three has its own separate area you just add them up and get 84.

Swap it to a union and the whole thing changes character. Instead of three separate areas you now have one area that all three names point at, so they are three labels stuck on the same drawer. The drawer only has to be big enough for the roomiest of the three, which is one of the 40-letter rows, so 40 is the whole size. That also wrecks the lines above, because each one shoves its stuff into that same drawer and flattens whatever was already there: the title goes in, the author lands on top and the title is gone, then the 3 goes in and sits on the front of the author. When you print, the letters part comes out as nonsense and the number comes out as 3, because 3 was the last thing put in the drawer. Since a book has to hold onto its title and its author and its count all at the same moment, the drawer arrangement is no good for it, and it only suits things where you know only one of them is ever in use.

struct LibraryBook {
    char title[40];
    char author[40];
    int copies;
};

struct LibraryBook b;
b.title = "Things Fall Apart";
b.author = "Chinua Achebe";
b.copies = 3;
printf("%s: %d copies\n", b.title, b.copies);

The dot is how each member is reached, for setting a value and for reading one back, and the conversion specifiers in the printf follow the types of the members: %s for the two character arrays and %d for the integer count.

Size: 40 + 40 + 4 = 84 bytes. Every member of a structure is given its own storage, laid out in order, so the size of the structure is just the sum of the sizes of its members.

If struct were replaced by union, the three members would no longer have separate storage. They would all begin at the same address, so title, author and copies become three names for a single block, and the size falls to 40 bytes, the size of the largest member, since a union need only be large enough to hold its biggest member. The statements would then overwrite one another: the title is written into the block, the author is written over the top of it, and the assignment to copies puts a four-byte integer over the first bytes of what is there. The printf would show rubbish where the title should be and 3 for the copies, because copies was the last thing written. A structure is therefore the right choice here, because a book has to hold a title, an author and a count simultaneously, which a union cannot do.
